    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The p53 tumor suppressor is a central component of the DNA damage response, mediating its effect on cell cycle progression and cell death through a myriad of gene expression regulation patterns. A novel long noncoding RNA, DINO, has been recently discovered as an intricate part of the p53 damage response pathway, providing a feed-forward mechanism for p53 activation and stabilization following sustained DNA damage. While DINO promotes post-translational modifications of the p53 C-terminal domain in order to enhance protein stability, it is not yet know whether the post-translation modifications can impact the binding of p53 and DINO. In this study, we set out to identify the amino acid residues critical for p53’s binding of DINO. We were motivated by the observations that the basic residues of the C-terminus, which are likely involved DINO binding, are subjected to extensive post-translational modifications. We generated eighteen point mutations of p53, selectively targeting basic residues in the p53 C terminal domain to identify their role facilitating binding of DINO and p53. Here, we identify six basic lysine residues in p53’s C-terminal domain that we hypothesized to impart critical function in RNA mediated binding of p53. Using site-directed mutagenesis, we successfully synthesized 18 mutant constructs in total, with mutations to alanine, glutamine, and lysine at each of the six lysine residues. To best characterize the functionality of these various p53 mutants, we developed a high-throughput method of RNA immunoprecipitation to characterize their impact on the protein’s ability to bind DINO. qRT-PCR analysis provided us with initial insight into the success of this in vitro RNA binding assay, imparting us with a foundation to better assess the role of post-translation modification of basic lysine residues in p53’s C-terminal domain in the future. A deeper understanding of how p53 modifications impact lncRNA interaction provide a deeper understanding of the mechanism by which p53 post-translational modification “barcode” can elicit distinct cellular phenomenon (1).",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
